---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleCard from '../components/ArticleCard.astro';
import SectionHeading from '../components/SectionHeading.astro';
import PlaceholderArt from '../components/PlaceholderArt.astro';
// No invented publication date. An approved date can be added at content integration.
---
<BaseLayout title="Sample article layout" description="A clearly marked sample article for design review. Neutral demonstration text, a fictional byline, and original placeholder artwork.">
  <article class="sample-article shell" aria-labelledby="article-title">
    <header class="article-header reading-width">
      <a class="text-link breadcrumb" href="/">← Back to the homepage prototype</a>
      <p class="eyebrow">Sample article <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> Design demonstration</p>
      <h1 id="article-title">Room to read,<br /><em>space to think.</em></h1>
      <p class="article-deck">An example of how a long-form story can feel: considered, comfortable, and easy to follow.</p>
      <div class="article-byline"><span class="author-monogram" aria-hidden="true">EW</span><div><p>By <strong>Example Writer</strong></p><p class="card-meta">Fictional byline <span aria-hidden="true">·</span> No publication date assigned</p></div></div>
      <p class="sample-disclosure">This page is a layout sample, not publishable journalism. All text below is neutral demonstration copy.</p>
    </header>
    <figure class="article-hero"><PlaceholderArt variant="paper" label="Hero image placeholder" /><figcaption>Figure placeholder. Original geometric artwork demonstrates the image area; caption and credit styling appear here.</figcaption></figure>
    <div class="article-body reading-width">
      <p class="opening-paragraph">A page begins with a little space. Before the first paragraph, there is a headline, a pause, and an invitation to read. This sample uses ordinary words to make the shape of a long article visible. It does not describe a real event, interview, or reported finding.</p>
      <p>The width of a paragraph changes the pace of reading. A comfortable line leaves enough room for a complete thought while making the next line easy to find. Here, the text column stays within a measured width, and the margins become smaller as the screen narrows.</p>
      <h2>A rhythm for reading</h2>
      <p>Headings offer a place to pause. They also help a reader return to a passage without retracing every sentence. The words in this section are deliberately simple, so the spacing, type, and hierarchy can be reviewed on their own.</p>
      <p>Short paragraphs can sit beside longer ones without losing a shared rhythm. The intention is to give each idea enough room, with a clear distinction between the article itself and supporting information such as captions or credits.</p>
      <blockquote><p>Good reading begins with room for the words.</p><cite>Original demonstration text — not an attributed quotation</cite></blockquote>
      <h2>Giving images their context</h2>
      <p>An image and its caption belong together. A caption can explain what an approved image shows, while a separate credit identifies its creator. The abstract illustration below is a design placeholder, so it carries no claim about a person, place, or event.</p>
      <figure><PlaceholderArt variant="orbit" label="Inline image placeholder" /><figcaption>Inline figure demonstration. The caption stays close to the illustration and uses smaller, readable text.</figcaption></figure>
      <p>A future reported article may include links, lists, or additional sections. Those elements should appear when they help the reader. This sample includes a <a href="/#latest">link back to the latest-story layout</a> to demonstrate a visible, keyboard-accessible link within body text.</p>
      <h2>A clear place to finish</h2>
      <p>The final paragraph brings the reading area to a natural close. Related cards follow in a separate section, giving the reader another place to go without interrupting the body of the article.</p>
      <p class="article-end">End of demonstration <span aria-hidden="true">■</span></p>
    </div>
  </article>
  <section class="shell section-block related-section" aria-labelledby="related-title">
    <SectionHeading id="related-title" title="Continue exploring" eyebrow="Related layout samples" />
    <div class="two-column-grid">
      <ArticleCard category="Homepage" title="The reading desk" description="Return to the latest-story cards on the homepage prototype." href="/#latest" layout="text" />
      <ArticleCard category="Visual essay" title="A space for images" description="Review the photo-feature section and its placeholder artwork." href="/#photo-features" layout="text" />
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
